      {
        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 14,
        "titulo": "Ein, mein, kein + adjetivo, passo a passo",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: «ein», «eine», «mein», «kein»… do not show the case in THREE places: masculine nominative («ein»), neuter nominative («ein») and neuter accusative («ein»). In those places the ADJECTIVE takes over the job and shows the case: «ein großer Mann» (-er), «ein kleines Kind» (-es). STEP 2: in the other places, the article already shows the case, and the adjective takes «-e» or «-en» just as after «der»: «eine kleine Frau», «einen großen Mann». STEP 3: nominative: ein großer Mann (-er), eine kleine Frau (-e), ein neues Auto (-es), meine neuen Autos (-en). STEP 4: accusative: einen großen Mann (-en), eine kleine Frau (-e), ein neues Auto (-es), meine neuen Autos (-en). STEP 5: rule of thumb: «-er» and «-es» only appear when «ein» has no ending: masculine nominative and neuter nominative and accusative.",
        "comparacao": "English has nothing like this. The logic is simple: German wants the case to show up ONCE; if the article (ein) did not show it, the adjective does.",
        "exemplos": [
          {
            "ru": "Das ist ein netter Mann.",
            "pt": "This is a nice man."
